1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,680
It's the world's first stadium, a 2,000-year-old masterpiece that contemporary designers still regard with awe.

2
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:17,300
It may have held as many as 80,000 people and shook for centuries with a savage entertainment that continues to fascinate and appall.

3
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,720
A thousand gladiators are said to have died here in a single day.

4
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,500
Now, the Coliseum on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:48,583 --> 00:00:51,900
Nothing evokes the greatness and brutality of ancient Rome

6
00:00:51,901 --> 00:00:54,720
better than the fallen giant known as the Colosseum.

7
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:57,760
This was once the heartbeat of an empire

8
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:01,420
where huge crowds reveled in gladiatorial combat

9
00:01:01,421 --> 00:01:04,900
that reflected and reinforced a larger Roman drama

10
00:01:04,901 --> 00:01:09,080
of warfare and conquest, of life and death.

11
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,080
In terms of size and scale,

12
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:16,420
this 2,000-year-old stadium with its four levels and 80 entrances

13
00:01:16,421 --> 00:01:18,880
would be impressive if it were built today.

14
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:24,340
With a capacity that some experts put as high as 80,000,

15
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:28,080
The Colosseum is still one of the largest stadiums in the world,

16
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:32,840
a structural wonder that was built nearly 2,000 years ahead of its time.

17
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:42,060
This kind of theaters and amphitheaters were realized all over the world during the Roman dominion,

18
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:47,020
but no one ever reached this dimension.

19
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:52,000
From the structural point of view, that was a very relevant accomplishment.

20
00:01:52,001 --> 00:02:01,480
I'm sure it was very impressive 2,000 years ago as it is today, perhaps even more so, because now we are used to it through our information channels.

21
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:07,660
But there was no television 2,000 years ago, so people, when they came to Rome, that is the first time they actually saw the whole building.

22
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:09,920
So I think it must have been very great.

23
00:02:11,986 --> 00:02:22,540
There's a wonderful line that's attributed to the venerable Bede, living around 700, who said that as long as the Colosseum stands, Rome will stand, and when the

24
00:02:22,541 --> 00:02:27,128
Colosseum falls, Rome will fall, and when Rome falls, the world falls.

25
00:02:27,129 --> 00:02:31,520
You can't do any better than that as far as a building's concerned.

26
00:02:31,521 --> 00:02:39,780
Over 160 feet high, 617 feet long, and 512 feet wide,

27
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:43,600
the Coliseum was state-of-the-art until very recent times,

28
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,720
featuring a retractable roof that blocked out the sun

29
00:02:46,721 --> 00:02:50,700
and a maze of sophisticated underground staging devices,

30
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:53,160
including elevators and trap doors,

31
00:02:53,161 --> 00:02:57,420
that made the production of the most spectacular gladiatorial displays possible.

32
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:01,220
The Coliseum, of course, is more than a stadium.

33
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:09,020
It's an echo chamber that reverberates through history with a roar of a crowd caught up in the bloody spectacle of gladiatorial games.

34
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:17,740
When you compare it to a building like the Pantheon, for example, or the Vatican, I mean, there's a quietness about those buildings.

35
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,980
But quiet is not what you think when you walk up to the Colosseum.

36
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:26,440
You can just hear 50,000 people and see them streaming to that place.

37
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:28,880
It is so clearly a gathering place.

38
00:03:28,881 --> 00:03:34,440
and, again, an incredible memory that the building has of all of the events that happened there.

39
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,700
Tens of thousands of gladiators died here,

40
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:41,500
along with vast numbers of prisoners of war and criminals

41
00:03:41,501 --> 00:03:44,620
who were executed in horrifically varied ways.

42
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:51,360
Countless lions, tigers, elephants, giraffes, and other exotic animals were also slaughtered.

43
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:55,440
The world still struggles to reconcile how Rome,

44
00:03:55,441 --> 00:03:58,840
which saw itself as the great civilizing power of its time,

45
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:04,940
could revel in killing on such a scale for nearly five centuries as a form of entertainment.

46
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:10,020
I believe that the Romans would have enjoyed it.

47
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:11,600
And it was exciting.

48
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:16,740
It's a bit like we look now at the television and we had all these terminators killing people,

49
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:21,780
criminals around the world, and we think it is, of course, a virtual reality and we don't think about it.

50
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:24,420
I don't think the Romans thought that it was more than that.

51
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:26,860
I think it is just another television show.

52
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:33,260
The Colosseum wasn't called the Colosseum until many centuries after it was built.

53
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,800
In its prime, it was called the Flavian Amphitheater,

54
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:39,920
in honor of the imperial family that authorized its construction.

55
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:45,540
It borrowed the name Colosseum long after it closed,

56
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:51,180
from an enormous 120-foot statue of Nero called the Colossus that stood nearby.

57
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:54,120
Nothing remains of the statue,

58
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:58,680
but a fragment of the massive pedestal that once held it high is still in place.

59
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,400
just outside the Colosseum's western facade.

60
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:07,480
The amphitheater, or double theater,

61
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:12,000
was invented specifically to give people a better view of gladiatorial games.

62
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:17,680
Wooden amphitheaters probably appeared as early as the 3rd century B.C.

63
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,080
But the first stone amphitheater,

64
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:25,040
a simple structure that had four entrances and could hold 12,000 people,

65
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:27,720
was built in Pompeii around 70 B.C.

66
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:39,248
One hundred years later, Rome still did not have a large permanent amphitheater, which was strange because Rome was the largest city in the world, by far, with over a

67
00:05:39,249 --> 00:05:44,120
million inhabitants, more than twice as many as Alexandria, its nearest rival.

68
00:05:44,121 --> 00:05:49,360
Rome was in fact in many ways more like a modern city than an ancient one

69
00:05:49,361 --> 00:05:55,080
incredibly congested, crammed with theaters, temples, palaces and other great structures

70
00:05:55,081 --> 00:05:58,700
and teeming with a restless population that was hungry for diversion

71
00:05:58,701 --> 00:06:07,800
Around 70 AD the new emperor Vespasian decided to provide some of that diversion

72
00:06:07,801 --> 00:06:10,620
by building an amphitheater worthy of the imperial capital

73
00:06:10,621 --> 00:06:16,620
The architects who designed the new amphitheater faced an enormous set of challenges.

74
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,640
To hold so many people, the stadium had to be four levels high,

75
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:24,860
with seating on the first three and standing room at the top.

76
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:28,880
The outer facade would be classical, even by ancient standards,

77
00:06:28,881 --> 00:06:34,920
with repeating tiers of arches and decorated columns laid out according to traditional rules of design.

78
00:06:34,921 --> 00:06:42,780
80 entrances, 76 of them numbered, would make it easy for the huge crowds to get in and out of the amphitheater.

79
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:49,820
Everything about the Coliseum's inner design was also organized with traffic flow in mind.

80
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:58,040
A series of concentric walls, including the outer facade, would create lanes of traffic on all four levels.

81
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:04,900
Radial passageways corresponding to the entrances would make it easy for people to get to their seats.

82
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:08,600
and a system of stairways which tie the whole thing together.

83
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:15,800
One of the first and biggest challenges in building the Coliseum

84
00:07:15,801 --> 00:07:20,520
was quarrying and hauling the stone for the stadium's 160-foot outer wall.

85
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:27,900
A local form of limestone called travertine was cut into 8-by-3-foot blocks

86
00:07:27,901 --> 00:07:31,620
and carried 20 miles from a quarry in the neighboring town of Tivoli.

87
00:07:31,621 --> 00:07:36,780
Tivoli still exists, as do its ancient quarries

88
00:07:36,781 --> 00:07:41,520
which yielded 100,000 cubic feet of travertine for the Colosseum facade

89
00:07:41,521 --> 00:07:47,660
I cannot conceive what 100,000 cubic feet would look like

90
00:07:47,661 --> 00:07:53,160
but it sounds absolutely enormous if you think about it

91
00:07:53,161 --> 00:08:00,320
this would be acres of stone in that one building

92
00:08:00,321 --> 00:08:06,640
The Romans built a special road just to transport the Colosseum stone.

93
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:10,280
And 200 ox carts made the trip each day.

94
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:13,780
240,000 cart loads in all.

95
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:20,060
The other major materials used to build the Colosseum were iron.

96
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,920
300 tons of which were used to clamp the blocks into place.

97
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:27,000
Tufa, a light but strong local stone.

98
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:28,320
And brick.

99
00:08:28,321 --> 00:08:39,220
It's an integrated system of materials with different structural capacities used in a hierarchical way.

100
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:46,720
They've used travertine piers. Travertine is a kind of limestone.

101
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:53,920
They've used two far walls, a volcanic stone, not as good as travertine, but still pretty good.

102
00:08:53,921 --> 00:08:56,540
than brick-faced concrete.

103
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:00,420
So this system stitched the whole building together

104
00:09:00,421 --> 00:09:04,480
in a fully integrated hall.

105
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:10,060
Very little is really known about the equipment

106
00:09:10,061 --> 00:09:11,960
the Romans used to build the Colosseum.

107
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:15,840
How, for example, could they lift such heavy blocks of stone

108
00:09:15,841 --> 00:09:16,640
to such a height?

109
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:23,520
One tantalizing image from the gravestone of a contractor

110
00:09:23,521 --> 00:09:25,860
who was commissioned to build a quarter of the Coliseum,

111
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:27,280
gives us a glimpse.

112
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:32,040
It is a very impressive scene of a massive crane

113
00:09:32,041 --> 00:09:36,560
which is hauling blocks up with pulleys.

114
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:41,680
The crane is actually operated by a kind of revolving wheel

115
00:09:41,681 --> 00:09:45,720
with slaves treading a treadmill type of arrangement

116
00:09:45,721 --> 00:09:50,060
to move, to rotate the crane into position.

117
00:09:50,061 --> 00:09:54,460
The Colosseum took about 10 years to build.

118
00:09:55,160 --> 00:10:00,700
Not a bad speed by contemporary standards, but an astonishing one 2,000 years ago.

119
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:09,420
They didn't have computers, and they didn't have electrical tools or so, and the building is gigantic.

120
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:17,240
And any number of little errors and glitches must have happened that slowed down the construction,

121
00:10:17,241 --> 00:10:23,240
but despite all that, they were able to coordinate their building efforts.

122
00:10:24,460 --> 00:10:27,880
Such efforts would soon be put to an equally difficult test.

123
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:31,720
Up next, the labyrinthine underworld of the Colosseum.

124
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:37,360
Archaeologists have found evidence of 110 drinking fountains

125
00:10:37,361 --> 00:10:40,520
and two very large lavatories in the Colosseum.

126
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:43,400
The Colosseum will return on Modern Marvels.

127
00:10:43,401 --> 00:10:46,500
we now return to the Colosseum

128
00:10:46,501 --> 00:10:49,080
on Modern Marvels

129
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:57,180
gladiators once moved through these narrow passageways

130
00:10:57,181 --> 00:11:00,040
along with stagehands, animal handlers

131
00:11:00,041 --> 00:11:02,140
lions, crocodiles, elephants

132
00:11:02,141 --> 00:11:06,180
and other wild beasts captured from the far reaches of the Roman Empire

133
00:11:06,181 --> 00:11:10,120
this is the underworld of the Colosseum

134
00:11:10,121 --> 00:11:13,940
two floors worth of twisting corridors and cul-de-sacs

135
00:11:13,941 --> 00:11:16,560
that used to be hidden beneath the arena floor

136
00:11:16,561 --> 00:11:19,900
and at once bustled with all the behind-the-scenes activity

137
00:11:19,901 --> 00:11:24,060
that made the gladiatorial games and other deadly spectacles possible.

138
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:29,260
Domitian, the younger son of the emperor of Aspasian,

139
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:32,760
built this netherworld called the Hypogeum, or Underground,

140
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:35,480
when he became emperor in 81 AD.

141
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:39,840
We have to think of a normal theater.

142
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:42,320
Big activities, many people.

143
00:11:42,321 --> 00:11:45,460
Here, someone is running there.

144
00:11:45,660 --> 00:11:47,320
Something else is moving ropes.

145
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:52,380
Something else is preparing costumes.

146
00:11:52,620 --> 00:12:04,560
So let's imagine this very crowded atmosphere down here in narrow spaces like this one.

147
00:12:04,740 --> 00:12:09,000
The staging, the idea that it truly is a labyrinth is amazing

148
00:12:09,001 --> 00:12:14,440
because it's not only an engineering marvel, it's an operational marvel.

149
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:21,040
It truly was this intricate set of passageways that helped to host the event

150
00:12:21,041 --> 00:12:23,220
and enhance the spectacle of it.

151
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:26,960
But interestingly enough, if you peeled off the basketball floor

152
00:12:26,961 --> 00:12:30,140
or the hockey floor of a modern-day arena, it would look pretty much the same,

153
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:32,060
be built out of slightly different materials.

154
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:37,580
Archaeologists have been piecing together the complex story

155
00:12:37,581 --> 00:12:39,060
of the Colosseum substructure

156
00:12:39,061 --> 00:12:42,660
since scientific excavation began in the 1870s.

157
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:44,920
A fascinating part of that story

158
00:12:44,921 --> 00:12:46,080
involves the elevators

159
00:12:46,081 --> 00:12:48,280
that carried gladiators, animals, and scenery

160
00:12:48,281 --> 00:12:49,280
to the arena floor.

161
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:54,080
Thirty-two elevators,

162
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:55,900
specially designed for lions,

163
00:12:56,180 --> 00:12:57,800
panthers, and other dangerous animals,

164
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,660
ringed the arena at the level of the hypogeum.

165
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:05,180
They were brought up from the lower floor in cages,

166
00:13:05,181 --> 00:13:09,080
probably operated by some kind of counterpoise system

167
00:13:09,081 --> 00:13:13,660
in which the weight is released and in the process the cage rises.

168
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:18,060
The animal emerged and attracted by the light

169
00:13:18,061 --> 00:13:22,340
and no doubt there was some kind of bait at the top of the ramp

170
00:13:22,341 --> 00:13:24,620
which might have encouraged him to charge.

171
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:27,220
And if things were going well,

172
00:13:27,221 --> 00:13:32,020
it was possible to get all these animals emerging simultaneously.

173
00:13:32,021 --> 00:13:38,060
Larger hinged platforms or ramps called pegmata were located in the center of the arena.

174
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:41,800
Counterweights were used to lift elephants and other massive animals,

175
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:45,480
as well as spectacular scenery including forests and mountains.

176
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:52,200
We have no exact idea about how they could recreate in a few seconds,

177
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:55,080
for instance, the volcano Vesuvio.

178
00:13:55,081 --> 00:14:05,620
We know that in 3rd century AD, the volcano was rebuilt inside the arena of the Colosseum,

179
00:14:05,960 --> 00:14:11,320
and that it appeared suddenly in a triumph of fire and lava.

180
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:12,520
It's unbelievable.

181
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:14,820
But they did it.

182
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:21,300
In addition to the underground, Domitian added something to the Colosseum

183
00:14:21,301 --> 00:14:24,520
that modern stadium spectators only began to grow accustomed to

184
00:14:24,521 --> 00:14:26,200
in the last half of the 20th century.

185
00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:28,300
A retractable roof.

186
00:14:30,220 --> 00:14:32,700
The velarium, as it was called, was made of cloth

187
00:14:32,701 --> 00:14:36,020
and unfurled by a detachment of 300 imperial sailors

188
00:14:36,021 --> 00:14:38,600
permanently stationed outside the Colosseum.

189
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:44,260
The Owning protected spectators from the burning Mediterranean sun.

190
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:50,360
Waiting for hours all day in the summer is not easy.

191
00:14:50,361 --> 00:15:04,361
So the velarium was a great gift of the Flavian Empire to give more beauty, even more beauty, more magnificence to the stage, to the caviar, and to the Romans.

192
00:15:06,620 --> 00:15:16,908
While the velarium itself has long vanished, physical evidence attesting to its existence is easy to find, in the form of brackets that circle the Colosseum facade,

193
00:15:16,909 --> 00:15:18,100
240 of them in all.

194
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:24,800
Each bracket held a tall beam of wood, much like the mast of a ship.

195
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:31,180
At a signal, the sailors pulled ropes, stretching a 24-ton colored canvas in place.

196
00:15:31,980 --> 00:15:37,300
The canvas covered two-thirds of the arena, leaving the center above the arena floor exposed.

197
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,880
Traces of the stairways the sailors used to get to their positions inside the Coliseum Wall can still be seen.

198
00:15:45,881 --> 00:15:52,360
Not much is definitively known about how the velarium worked.

199
00:15:52,740 --> 00:15:55,900
Indeed, archaeologists have yet to postulate a credible design

200
00:15:55,901 --> 00:15:58,440
to explain how such a large awning could work,

201
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:00,260
given the wind stresses involved.

202
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:03,980
We know how broadly the velarium worked,

203
00:16:03,981 --> 00:16:08,000
that the system of ropes and pulleys could open it and close it

204
00:16:08,001 --> 00:16:12,460
and probably open and close different parts separately,

205
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:15,760
that you didn't have to open the whole thing at once and close it all.

206
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:18,340
But these are still generalities.

207
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:28,220
Exactly how they were attached to the valabrum and where the sailors stood and how they pulled it, that we don't know.

208
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:37,820
The Coliseum opened in 80 AD in spectacular style with 100 days of gladiatorial games.

209
00:16:39,820 --> 00:16:45,800
5,000 animals and an unknown number of men would die during a single day of the inaugural blood fest.

210
00:16:45,880 --> 00:16:55,920
For the first two years of the Colosseum's life, before Domitian added the Hypogeum, gladiators actually fought on water in mock recreations of famous naval battles.

211
00:16:55,921 --> 00:17:05,020
the arena that in the first phase was completely wooden was dismantled in few hours and then the

212
00:17:05,021 --> 00:17:12,400
basin was flooded because it was connected with an aqueduct and in few hours spectators could

213
00:17:12,401 --> 00:17:21,580
observe sheep fighting inside the coliseum admission to the coliseum was free and tickets

214
00:17:21,581 --> 00:17:23,100
in the form of pottery shards

215
00:17:23,101 --> 00:17:25,140
were distributed by city officials.

216
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:27,180
A number on the ticket

217
00:17:27,181 --> 00:17:29,580
indicated which of the arched entrances to go through.

218
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:36,000
Seating was based strictly on social class.

219
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:38,220
The emperor and VIPs

220
00:17:38,221 --> 00:17:41,040
sat on a 13-foot-high stepped marble platform

221
00:17:41,041 --> 00:17:41,960
at the first level.

222
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:44,560
Iron bars and netting protected them

223
00:17:44,561 --> 00:17:46,320
from animals, weapons, and gore.

224
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:49,820
Middle-class citizens sat on the second tier.

225
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:52,200
Poorer citizens sat on the third.

226
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:56,260
The top level with steep wooden bleachers and standing room at the back

227
00:17:56,261 --> 00:17:59,420
was reserved for women, the very poor, and slaves.

228
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:05,360
Seats were made of marble, only a few fragments of which remained.

229
00:18:08,160 --> 00:18:13,640
As a people mover, able to circulate some of the largest crowds in the ancient world with speed and ease,

230
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:15,900
the Colosseum proved to be phenomenal.

231
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:22,680
Modern estimations have been something like you could empty that place within 12 minutes or something like that.

232
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:24,380
I mean, that's mind-boggling, isn't it?

233
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:27,680
That people could leave their seats, go to the nearest exit,

234
00:18:27,681 --> 00:18:32,320
and from that exit, without crossing over to the second exit's line,

235
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:42,340
could directly go down steps and ramps and exit all along the 360-degree circumference of the building

236
00:18:42,341 --> 00:18:46,380
and get dispersed into the great city.

237
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:52,920
And that really was the great achievement of planning of the Colosseum.

238
00:18:55,540 --> 00:19:00,480
The people who poured into the Colosseum no doubt marveled at the magnificent structure

239
00:19:00,481 --> 00:19:02,040
as they raced to their seats.

240
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:06,220
But what they were really there for was to see the action of the games.

241
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:11,520
Up next, gladiators, animals, and executions in the arena.

242
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:19,360
The Colosseum was financed with the plunder of Jerusalem, which Rome conquered after a long siege in 70 AD.

243
00:19:20,620 --> 00:19:23,480
The Colosseum will return on Modern Marvelous.

244
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:30,600
The Colosseum was not the only amphitheater in Rome.

245
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:39,160
In fact, another arena was right next door, the Ludus Magnus, in which gladiators who fought in the Colosseum lived and trained.

246
00:19:39,161 --> 00:19:45,420
the fighters along with their trainers and doctors lived in barracks around the perimeter of the

247
00:19:45,421 --> 00:19:51,320
ludus magnus three thousand people could watch as they practiced on a fighting field that was

248
00:19:51,321 --> 00:19:56,700
less than a third smaller than the one in the coliseum consider that the size of the ludus

249
00:19:56,701 --> 00:20:03,000
magnus is more or less the same size size of an amphitheater in a normal town of the empire

250
00:20:03,001 --> 00:20:08,540
we're in rome we have the coliseum other cities they have amphitheaters as big as our ludus

251
00:20:08,541 --> 00:20:09,241
Magnus.

252
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:13,420
An underground tunnel linked the Ludus Magnus to the Colosseum,

253
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:17,280
enabling the gladiators to make a dramatic entrance onto the field of battle.

254
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:22,320
By the time the Colosseum opened in 80 AD,

255
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:25,840
gladiatorial combat was the mass entertainment of its day.

256
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:29,280
But the roots of the gladiator go back to the 4th century B.C.

257
00:20:29,281 --> 00:20:33,220
and the Etruscans, a powerful civilization south of Rome.

258
00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:36,040
They seem to have developed some funerary games

259
00:20:36,041 --> 00:20:43,680
where a major warrior died, and then in memory of him, gladiators would fight to the death.

260
00:20:45,320 --> 00:20:48,240
Reflecting their religious origins and entertainment appeal,

261
00:20:48,620 --> 00:20:51,360
the Romans had two words for gladiatorial combat.

262
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:56,920
Munera, originally meaning rights in honor of the dead, and ludi, or games.

263
00:20:59,940 --> 00:21:03,920
The Colosseum, of course, was the ultimate stage for gladiatorial games.

264
00:21:03,921 --> 00:21:08,900
animal hunts, and other blood sport, which would last from dawn until dark.

265
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:15,140
Condemned criminals who were frequently crucified provided the first blood of the day.

266
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:19,100
To make the executions more entertaining,

267
00:21:19,820 --> 00:21:25,120
the victims were sometimes compelled to perform in special plays known as fatal charades.

268
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:30,880
The person who was to be executed was made to play a role in a drama.

269
00:21:30,881 --> 00:21:33,000
and of course at the end of the drama

270
00:21:33,001 --> 00:21:34,600
the person was going to die

271
00:21:34,601 --> 00:21:37,180
but as opposed to an actor on a stage

272
00:21:37,181 --> 00:21:38,560
the death was very real

273
00:21:38,561 --> 00:21:41,500
and the person was executed as the part of the drama.

274
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:45,680
The main event, signaled by a blast from trumpets

275
00:21:45,681 --> 00:21:47,320
involved the gladiators

276
00:21:47,321 --> 00:21:51,040
who specialized in 20 different styles and forms of fighting

277
00:21:51,041 --> 00:21:54,220
representing the far-flung enemies the Romans had conquered.

278
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:06,140
One of the most popular matchups pitted combatants called Rattari,

279
00:22:06,280 --> 00:22:09,120
who fought naked with just a trident and a net,

280
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:11,720
against the heavily armored Samnites,

281
00:22:12,340 --> 00:22:16,400
named after an Italian people whom the Romans conquered in the 3rd century B.C.

282
00:22:19,820 --> 00:22:23,640
A wounded gladiator would lay down his weapon and ask for his life.

283
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:27,560
He was judged by the crowd according to his bravery and skill.

284
00:22:27,561 --> 00:22:33,360
interestingly the thumbs up verdict of the crowd did not mean what most people think it meant

285
00:22:33,361 --> 00:22:41,600
thumbs up is not actually a success rather the thumb was up and in in towards the chest and that

286
00:22:41,601 --> 00:22:48,800
was the sign of deliver the final blow stab him in the chest you see or the other one was the thumb

287
00:22:48,801 --> 00:22:57,120
down and out and that indicated throw the sword down and let him off so those were the two options

288
00:22:57,121 --> 00:23:01,500
and it was up to the emperor to decide which it was to be.

289
00:23:03,420 --> 00:23:08,080
Through the years, artists have wrongly depicted the thumbs down sign as a death sentence.

290
00:23:09,220 --> 00:23:14,060
In any event, a fallen gladiator who was condemned did what he had taken an oath to do.

291
00:23:14,720 --> 00:23:18,580
He assumed the posture of one who was about to die and received the death blow.

292
00:23:22,040 --> 00:23:24,340
Two attendants then examined the body.

293
00:23:24,341 --> 00:23:30,900
he had to be checked that he wasn't faking it you see if you you might pretend you're dead and

294
00:23:30,901 --> 00:23:36,800
get off with it but just to ensure that that wasn't happening uh slave came along with a

295
00:23:36,801 --> 00:23:44,780
branding iron and uh if the if the corpse just lay prostrate with the the branding it uh that was

296
00:23:44,781 --> 00:23:53,580
pretty secure he was dead but his leg started to flail around and he screamed then he was faking

297
00:23:53,581 --> 00:23:58,000
it and of course he would be killed anyway just to make sure that the job was done.

298
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:03,720
The dead body was dragged through the porta libertinaria or gate of death.

299
00:24:06,480 --> 00:24:11,260
Contrary to popular notions, a gladiator match didn't always result in a death.

300
00:24:13,060 --> 00:24:17,880
Training and maintaining gladiators cost a lot of money and the institution of the games

301
00:24:17,881 --> 00:24:21,340
could not have survived if half the fighters died in every round of combat.

302
00:24:21,341 --> 00:24:30,920
In fact, talented gladiators probably fought like boxers today, twice a year, and had a reasonably good chance of a lengthy career.

303
00:24:32,140 --> 00:24:35,460
Funerary inscriptions of gladiators boast of dozens of fights.

304
00:24:38,300 --> 00:24:42,000
Great fighters were the superstars and sex symbols of their day.

305
00:24:42,940 --> 00:24:47,920
Their allure was so great that a number of free Romans voluntarily took the oath to fight to the death.

306
00:24:47,921 --> 00:24:54,760
and one emperor, Commodus, appeared in the Colosseum no less than 735 times.

307
00:24:57,780 --> 00:25:02,940
After the gladiators, exotic and dangerous animals were the main attraction in the Colosseum.

308
00:25:03,660 --> 00:25:08,060
Indeed, the Romans killed so many animals in the Colosseum and other amphitheaters of the empire

309
00:25:08,061 --> 00:25:12,120
that an entire industry of big game hunting and transport thrived,

310
00:25:12,121 --> 00:25:14,400
and whole species were exterminated,

311
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:17,960
like the North African elephant and the Caspian tiger.

312
00:25:19,860 --> 00:25:22,560
The animals were frequently used to kill prisoners

313
00:25:22,561 --> 00:25:25,840
who had been sentenced to die, odd bestias.

314
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:29,320
Sometimes they were massacred, hundreds at a time,

315
00:25:29,720 --> 00:25:34,580
in lavishly recreated hunts that reaffirmed the power of the empire for the hometown crown.

316
00:25:36,200 --> 00:25:39,120
This is the entitlement that the Roman people have.

317
00:25:39,121 --> 00:25:42,220
It's the success of their empire has made them so wealthy

318
00:25:42,221 --> 00:25:45,840
and has given them the kind of power that they can go to these other countries

319
00:25:45,841 --> 00:25:48,520
and say, you know, we're taking these animals from you

320
00:25:48,521 --> 00:25:50,080
and we're taking them back to Rome.

321
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:53,760
And then you just put them there in front of the audience and you kill them

322
00:25:53,761 --> 00:25:57,480
and you say, you people are worth it, you residents of Rome.

323
00:25:57,560 --> 00:25:59,260
You deserve this kind of entertainment.

324
00:26:00,220 --> 00:26:02,200
So much blood shed in the arena.

325
00:26:02,840 --> 00:26:06,240
Animal and human, all to the horse shouts of a huge crowd,

326
00:26:06,241 --> 00:26:08,280
is a form of mass entertainment.

327
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:12,900
How do we explain such bloody revels, such barbarous fun?

328
00:26:13,920 --> 00:26:17,200
Certainly the atmosphere inside the Colosseum must have been overwhelming,

329
00:26:17,940 --> 00:26:19,860
irresistible in its own gruesome way.

330
00:26:21,160 --> 00:26:23,808
No less a figure than the Christian philosopher St.

331
00:26:23,809 --> 00:26:26,040
Augustine testified to its seductive power,

332
00:26:26,660 --> 00:26:31,180
describing the struggle of a religious friend who was dragged against his will to the games.

333
00:26:31,181 --> 00:26:39,440
he shut his eyes and he tried to divert himself with his pure thoughts from the noise and the

334
00:26:39,441 --> 00:26:44,500
screaming and the cheering that was going on and the mayhem that was going on all around him and

335
00:26:44,501 --> 00:26:49,900
there was one almighty roar from the crowd and he just couldn't uh keep his eyes closed he had to

336
00:26:49,901 --> 00:26:55,800
see his curiosity got the better of him and that was the end of him he he became even more uh

337
00:26:55,801 --> 00:26:59,960
devoted to the arena and started to bring his friends along.

338
00:27:00,560 --> 00:27:05,640
Millions of tourists would mull just that question for centuries after the demise of the Games.

339
00:27:06,780 --> 00:27:09,720
Up next, the long afterlife of the Colosseum

340
00:27:09,721 --> 00:27:13,780
and emergency measures that would keep the monument from collapsing.

341
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:17,980
Contrary to popular myth, there is no conclusive evidence

342
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:21,680
that Christians were ever fed to the lions or otherwise killed in the Colosseum,

343
00:27:22,060 --> 00:27:24,280
although they were put to death in many other amphitheaters.

344
00:27:24,281 --> 00:27:25,940
The Colosseum will return.

345
00:27:30,860 --> 00:27:36,040
The Roman Colosseum held standing room only crowds for gladiatorial death matches,

346
00:27:36,380 --> 00:27:40,600
animal massacres, and public executions for nearly five centuries.

347
00:27:42,400 --> 00:27:46,260
Imagine if Yankee Stadium or the Houston Astrodome had been around that long,

348
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:51,020
packed to the rafters with screaming fans since the 1500s.

349
00:27:51,021 --> 00:27:57,000
Inevitably, the Coliseum suffered its share of disasters over such a long span.

350
00:27:57,940 --> 00:28:03,300
Perhaps the greatest was caused by a fire that gutted the stadium in 217 A.D.,

351
00:28:03,301 --> 00:28:06,380
destroying the roof, the floor, and the underground substructure.

352
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:11,680
The fire burned so hot, it also melted much of the Coliseum limestone,

353
00:28:12,140 --> 00:28:17,100
and it took 20 years to reconstruct the amphitheater, twice the time it took to build it.

354
00:28:17,101 --> 00:28:21,060
The renovation looked perfect from the outside,

355
00:28:21,460 --> 00:28:27,280
but large parts of the Colosseum facade were rebuilt with fragments of travertine that survived the blaze.

356
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:33,700
Earthquakes which shook the building every century also caused serious damage.

357
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:37,200
After a particularly strong quake in the 5th century,

358
00:28:37,840 --> 00:28:44,000
wealthy citizens who paid for the repairs proudly inscribed their names on huge blocks around the rim of the arena.

359
00:28:44,920 --> 00:28:49,280
The inscription was a monumental inscription with very, very big letters

360
00:28:49,281 --> 00:28:54,260
carved on marble blocks that were put all around the arena.

361
00:28:54,940 --> 00:28:57,700
And the inscription was in two copies

362
00:28:57,701 --> 00:29:02,400
so that the public could read it from each side of the amphitheater.

363
00:29:02,401 --> 00:29:06,180
The Colosseum's glory days were over by the late 4th century

364
00:29:06,181 --> 00:29:11,180
as both the gladiatorial games and the Roman Empire began to decline.

365
00:29:11,181 --> 00:29:16,060
Rome no longer had the resources to mount such lavish entertainments.

366
00:29:16,600 --> 00:29:21,460
It would be nice to think that this decline was somehow correlative to the rise of Christianity

367
00:29:21,461 --> 00:29:24,700
at the same period in the 4th and the 5th century A.D.

368
00:29:24,701 --> 00:29:27,880
But in fact it was probably largely a financial matter

369
00:29:27,881 --> 00:29:31,420
that these games got to be much too expensive to produce

370
00:29:31,421 --> 00:29:35,440
at a time when the economy of the Roman Empire was declining.

371
00:29:35,441 --> 00:29:41,440
but the rise of Christianity probably also played a role in silencing the Colosseum

372
00:29:41,441 --> 00:29:47,620
Emperor Constantine made Christianity the official religion of the empire in 313 AD

373
00:29:47,621 --> 00:29:50,980
and Christians were supposed to abhor gladiatorial games

374
00:29:50,981 --> 00:29:55,660
in 391 AD a Greek monk named Telemachus

375
00:29:55,661 --> 00:30:00,120
flung himself into the arena and tried to separate two gladiators in mid-fight

376
00:30:00,121 --> 00:30:04,300
the inflamed audience stoned Telemachus to death

377
00:30:04,301 --> 00:30:09,020
But the games were finally banned 13 years later in 404 AD.

378
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:15,360
The Colosseum stayed open for another century, attracting smaller and smaller crowds

379
00:30:15,361 --> 00:30:22,660
and offering increasingly tawdry animal fights, featuring domestic animals like dogs and cocks.

380
00:30:23,480 --> 00:30:31,200
That means that the idea itself of the show inside the Colosseum has greatly changed.

381
00:30:31,201 --> 00:30:45,201
In the final phases of life at Colosseum, we should imagine these shows very close to our idea of circus, more than the classical idea of man fighting a tiger.

382
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:52,380
The last recorded show involving wild beasts took place in 523 AD.

383
00:30:53,720 --> 00:31:00,860
The long life of the Colosseum as an active stadium offering bloody spectacle to screaming fans was over.

384
00:31:01,200 --> 00:31:10,940
But the Colosseum's second, much longer life, as a legendary ruin that would be put to all manner of uses and abuses, was about to begin.

385
00:31:11,920 --> 00:31:23,100
As Rome shrank 40-fold to a city of just 25,000, a series of powerful families took over the Colosseum in the 12th century, turning it into a private fortress.

386
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:29,560
Any building that was secular was fair game to be transformed into a fortress.

387
00:31:29,561 --> 00:31:33,160
and amphitheaters are ideal

388
00:31:33,161 --> 00:31:36,780
because all you need to do is block up the arched entrances

389
00:31:36,781 --> 00:31:38,840
all the way around

390
00:31:38,841 --> 00:31:42,260
and you've got yourselves a magnificent fortress.

391
00:31:42,920 --> 00:31:45,720
You've saved a lot of space where you don't have to build a wall.

392
00:31:47,260 --> 00:31:49,920
The Colosseum's fortress days came to a sudden end

393
00:31:49,921 --> 00:31:53,860
when an earthquake in 1349 knocked down the southern facade

394
00:31:53,861 --> 00:31:56,240
which originally extended to this line.

395
00:31:57,180 --> 00:32:01,100
The collapsed wall created a giant quarry of high-grade limestone

396
00:32:01,101 --> 00:32:04,060
that local builders immediately began to plunder.

397
00:32:05,320 --> 00:32:09,520
There were thousands and thousands of cartloads of stone material

398
00:32:09,521 --> 00:32:13,700
which was sold by the popes to private contractors.

399
00:32:14,340 --> 00:32:17,380
A large part of Renaissance Rome, for example,

400
00:32:17,381 --> 00:32:20,560
was built out of the material of the Colosseum.

401
00:32:21,340 --> 00:32:24,020
And it was certainly used for the Basilica of St. Peter's

402
00:32:24,021 --> 00:32:26,340
in the 15th and 16th centuries,

403
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:30,440
especially in the 16th century,

404
00:32:30,920 --> 00:32:34,080
and then also for other churches and major palaces.

405
00:32:34,760 --> 00:32:36,260
In addition to the stone,

406
00:32:36,980 --> 00:32:39,800
scavengers removed every bit of iron from the Colosseum,

407
00:32:40,320 --> 00:32:43,040
pulling out all the clamps that held the travertine in place

408
00:32:43,041 --> 00:32:45,040
and further destabilizing the structure.

409
00:32:45,940 --> 00:32:49,360
To this day, nothing holds the Colosseum stone together

410
00:32:49,361 --> 00:32:51,660
except gravity and good design.

411
00:32:54,020 --> 00:32:57,780
In the Middle Ages, the Colosseum was considered a haunted place,

412
00:32:58,320 --> 00:33:02,020
possessed by pagan demons and the spirits of those who died in the arena.

413
00:33:03,160 --> 00:33:08,820
It was used as a wool factory, a gunpowder plant, and a dumping ground for the city's manure.

414
00:33:10,800 --> 00:33:15,460
It was also a shelter for the homeless, who camped out under its vaulted arcades.

415
00:33:17,400 --> 00:33:23,320
In 1744, the Catholic Church decided to turn the Colosseum, evil spirits and all,

416
00:33:23,321 --> 00:33:24,960
into a Christian shrine

417
00:33:24,961 --> 00:33:28,260
in memory of the martyrs who had supposedly died inside.

418
00:33:29,360 --> 00:33:31,260
A chapel was built on the arena floor

419
00:33:31,261 --> 00:33:33,880
along with 24 stations of the cross.

420
00:33:34,860 --> 00:33:37,260
For a time, the Vatican seriously considered

421
00:33:37,261 --> 00:33:39,520
converting the entire stadium into a church.

422
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:43,960
An architect named Carlo Fontana designed a basilica.

423
00:33:46,600 --> 00:33:49,500
He also made the first detailed studies of the building

424
00:33:49,501 --> 00:33:53,060
as well as a reconstruction of what it looked like in its prime.

425
00:33:53,320 --> 00:34:02,420
By the end of the 18th century, the Colosseum was once again one of the most celebrated structures in the world,

426
00:34:02,920 --> 00:34:07,640
a spectacular ruin that was considered an essential stop on the European Grand Tour.

427
00:34:09,780 --> 00:34:13,420
Poets and painters in particular fell in love with this evocative giant,

428
00:34:13,900 --> 00:34:17,020
which became a great symbol of 19th century Romanticism.

429
00:34:17,021 --> 00:34:25,060
It was very romantic to take a walk inside the Colosseum, especially by night with the moon.

430
00:34:25,700 --> 00:34:39,700
This huge ruin represents the fall of an enormous empire, whose traces are still visible, but whose ruins are in a certain way forgotten.

431
00:34:40,140 --> 00:34:44,340
Despite such interest, the Colosseum was in terrible shape,

432
00:34:44,920 --> 00:34:49,680
and the eastern facade nearly collapsed after yet another earthquake in 1806.

433
00:34:50,480 --> 00:34:56,280
So that gave the last push to actually start the restoration work.

434
00:34:56,880 --> 00:35:00,520
So the first thing, what was done in 1806, 1807,

435
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:06,580
was to build a large brick mattress against this wall which was about to collapse

436
00:35:06,581 --> 00:35:09,780
and just fix it as it was.

437
00:35:10,140 --> 00:35:14,580
So this ancient structure with all the fractures which can be seen today,

438
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:20,480
it became like a monument not only of the Colosseum, but of its own condition as well.

439
00:35:21,140 --> 00:35:23,760
So it was in a very, very fragile condition.

440
00:35:23,761 --> 00:35:27,500
And if they had not done this buttress, that part of the Colosseum would have gone.

441
00:35:29,220 --> 00:35:32,980
Roman authorities installed another emergency buttress in 1826.

442
00:35:33,980 --> 00:35:35,640
This one was not merely a buttress.

443
00:35:35,641 --> 00:35:38,680
it also attempted to reconstruct part of the Colosseum,

444
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:41,040
including the crumbling southern facade.

445
00:35:43,240 --> 00:35:45,060
A third intervention, decades later,

446
00:35:45,560 --> 00:35:47,460
propped up the endangered western wall

447
00:35:47,461 --> 00:35:50,640
and rebuilt long vanished arches and columns

448
00:35:50,641 --> 00:35:53,700
with red bricks that clearly differentiate themselves

449
00:35:53,701 --> 00:35:55,080
from the original Travertine.

450
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:01,300
There would be many more changes in store for the Colosseum.

451
00:36:01,900 --> 00:36:04,880
Up next, a reconstruction of the arena floor

452
00:36:04,881 --> 00:36:07,880
and the return of spectators and drama.

453
00:36:10,280 --> 00:36:13,660
Most of the marble seating in the Colosseum was stolen in the Middle Ages

454
00:36:13,661 --> 00:36:17,160
and recycled in palaces and churches throughout Rome.

455
00:36:18,240 --> 00:36:20,640
The Colosseum will return on Modern Marvels.

456
00:36:23,260 --> 00:36:28,440
The ancient killing field of the Colosseum is today, surprisingly, full of life.

457
00:36:28,441 --> 00:36:33,220
but this greenery is nothing compared to the vegetation

458
00:36:33,221 --> 00:36:35,840
that flourished here in the 18th and 19th centuries

459
00:36:35,841 --> 00:36:39,100
every kind of flower

460
00:36:39,101 --> 00:36:42,100
herb and fruit could be found in the arena floor

461
00:36:42,101 --> 00:36:44,620
a freakish cornucopia of plant life

462
00:36:44,621 --> 00:36:46,780
that had no business thriving in Rome

463
00:36:46,781 --> 00:36:49,680
it was completely overgrown

464
00:36:49,681 --> 00:36:52,160
there were trees growing out of the walls

465
00:36:52,161 --> 00:36:53,540
there were bushes

466
00:36:53,541 --> 00:36:55,520
in fact there was a whole while

467
00:36:55,521 --> 00:36:57,780
the books had been written about the bird life

468
00:36:57,781 --> 00:37:02,360
and the vegetation that choked the interior of the Colosseum.

469
00:37:03,700 --> 00:37:06,920
The Colosseum flora was so varied and strange

470
00:37:06,921 --> 00:37:10,760
that botanists came to Rome to study and document the plants.

471
00:37:11,720 --> 00:37:15,240
Richard Deacon of England illustrated over 420 species.

472
00:37:16,760 --> 00:37:20,260
How could the Colosseum give birth to such a varied profusion of plants?

473
00:37:21,660 --> 00:37:25,440
Probably from seeds dropped from the fur of the animals that fought in the arena.

474
00:37:25,441 --> 00:37:31,660
or from the foreign spectators who brought native foods with them to eat during the games.

475
00:37:33,940 --> 00:37:37,740
Unfortunately, the plants, which added greatly to the Colosseum's romantic air,

476
00:37:38,260 --> 00:37:40,300
were undermining the foundations of the stadium,

477
00:37:40,800 --> 00:37:43,800
and every blade of grass was rooted out in 1870

478
00:37:43,801 --> 00:37:46,720
when the first major excavation of the ruin began.

479
00:37:48,180 --> 00:37:51,520
The 18th century stations of the cross were still in place

480
00:37:51,521 --> 00:37:57,240
as excavators began clearing away the debris of centuries that had filled in the underground passageways.

481
00:37:59,340 --> 00:38:05,380
They quickly unearthed some exciting fragments, including ancient statuary and column capitals.

482
00:38:10,300 --> 00:38:15,280
Many of the artifacts are now stored in an underground chamber, off-limits to the public.

483
00:38:15,281 --> 00:38:22,140
floral and mythological decorations like these once adorned the Colosseum stairways and walls

484
00:38:22,141 --> 00:38:30,580
graffiti like this laurel branch for a victorious gladiator named Amato

485
00:38:30,581 --> 00:38:33,120
was etched into the ancient stone

486
00:38:33,121 --> 00:38:38,360
more than anything the Colosseum was full of inscriptions

487
00:38:38,361 --> 00:38:43,220
mostly related to identifying exactly who had the right to sit in the best seats

488
00:38:43,221 --> 00:38:47,860
Behind me you can see an example of a senatorial inscription

489
00:38:47,861 --> 00:38:55,140
that means a stone where they carved the names of the senators

490
00:38:55,141 --> 00:39:00,500
who had reserved seats in the first rows of the amphitheater.

491
00:39:01,100 --> 00:39:03,980
And on this stone you can read the names of three senators.

492
00:39:05,340 --> 00:39:10,540
You can see also the names of senators who had these seats before

493
00:39:10,541 --> 00:39:16,340
and whose names were erased once they died or they changed place.

494
00:39:18,740 --> 00:39:23,780
Political pressure to use the Colosseum as a nationalistic symbol increased in the 20th century

495
00:39:23,781 --> 00:39:27,200
when the fascist dictator Benito Mussolini came to power.

496
00:39:28,660 --> 00:39:31,800
Mussolini saw himself as a direct link to the emperors of old

497
00:39:31,801 --> 00:39:35,660
and he was so enamored with the power and the symbolism of the Colosseum

498
00:39:35,661 --> 00:39:39,180
that he actually held rallies on the exposed floor of the road.

499
00:39:40,540 --> 00:39:46,540
Ten years later, crowds of a different sort gathered under the ancient symbol of Rome

500
00:39:46,541 --> 00:39:51,540
to celebrate the fall of Mussolini and the end of World War II.

501
00:39:51,541 --> 00:39:59,540
After the war, archaeological work resumed, and excavators continued to make fascinating discoveries,

502
00:39:59,541 --> 00:40:06,540
unearthing the bones of people who died in the arena and the skull of a bear pierced by a gladiator's sword.

503
00:40:06,541 --> 00:40:13,020
Through the centuries, the Colosseum has managed to survive just about everything,

504
00:40:13,560 --> 00:40:17,560
from neglect to plunder to natural disasters to world war.

505
00:40:19,140 --> 00:40:23,840
Despite its aura of power and strength, however, the Colosseum is a fragile ruin.

506
00:40:25,160 --> 00:40:27,760
Less than half the original structure still stands,

507
00:40:28,460 --> 00:40:31,000
and earthquakes continue to pose a devastating threat.

508
00:40:31,680 --> 00:40:35,560
Another quake could bring this symbol of eternal Rome crashing down.

509
00:40:36,540 --> 00:40:42,500
It's delicate because the weight is huge and the dimensions are very large.

510
00:40:42,501 --> 00:40:48,920
As regards to the earthquake, the load is a bad component.

511
00:40:50,040 --> 00:40:57,980
And it was conceived to resist very well to vertical loads, to static loads.

512
00:40:58,500 --> 00:41:01,960
It's different when we have these huge horizontal forces.

513
00:41:01,961 --> 00:41:08,960
Italy's foremost quick proofing experts have been studying the Colosseum to determine how it will react to seismic stresses.

514
00:41:08,961 --> 00:41:15,960
We are carrying out very up-to-date and sophisticated structural analysis

515
00:41:15,961 --> 00:41:22,960
to well understand this complex behavior of the Colosseum under the seismic actions.

516
00:41:22,961 --> 00:41:30,960
And that is necessary to do as less as possible, because the interventions have to balance the minimum

517
00:41:30,961 --> 00:41:34,520
the minimum as regards the respect of the historic value

518
00:41:34,521 --> 00:41:38,340
and the maximum to obtain safety levels.

519
00:41:39,200 --> 00:41:44,060
Tourism, pollution, even the rumbling of traffic in the nearby subway,

520
00:41:44,720 --> 00:41:46,580
all pose hazards to the Colosseum.

521
00:41:47,140 --> 00:41:50,480
They may accelerate the aging process

522
00:41:50,481 --> 00:41:54,820
because the small vibration even can actually make it

523
00:41:54,821 --> 00:41:58,280
that the various cracks remain open,

524
00:41:58,780 --> 00:42:01,060
dust will go in, will increase it again.

525
00:42:01,400 --> 00:42:05,740
And if there is any earthquake or any other thing, that will make it worse.

526
00:42:07,200 --> 00:42:09,800
Not all the changes to the Colosseum have been damaging.

527
00:42:10,560 --> 00:42:14,040
People have long dreamed of reviving the Colosseum as a theater of sorts,

528
00:42:14,041 --> 00:42:17,120
and that dream was realized in the summer of 2000.

529
00:42:21,540 --> 00:42:27,020
The classical Greek tragedy Oedipus Rex was presented on a specially constructed stage

530
00:42:27,021 --> 00:42:30,880
that took five years and cost nearly a million dollars to implement and design.

531
00:42:38,680 --> 00:42:41,800
The stage is a partial reconstruction of the arena floor.

532
00:42:42,460 --> 00:42:44,840
It will eventually cover one half of the underground,

533
00:42:45,460 --> 00:42:48,340
affording tourists a gladiator's eye view of the great stadium.

534
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:55,600
You must have felt very much alone on that arena floor.

535
00:42:55,601 --> 00:43:02,600
50,000 people and every one of those people has come not because they're ever going to exhibit any compassion or pity for you,

536
00:43:02,640 --> 00:43:06,980
but because they want to see you suffer, they want to see you injured, they want to see you die.

537
00:43:07,440 --> 00:43:13,840
And to be there and looking up and seeing, you know, 50,000 pairs of eyes looking down at you,

538
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:20,720
I think that's one of the things that having the floor there is going to help visitors to imagine what it was like to be a victim.

539
00:43:20,721 --> 00:43:23,940
over 1900 years old

540
00:43:23,941 --> 00:43:25,940
1500 years a ruin

541
00:43:25,941 --> 00:43:28,660
the Colosseum continues to stir the imagination

542
00:43:28,661 --> 00:43:30,920
with its grandeur and its savage past

543
00:43:30,921 --> 00:43:32,380
from its birth

544
00:43:32,381 --> 00:43:34,460
it has been a symbol as much as a structure

545
00:43:34,461 --> 00:43:36,360
a symbol of antiquity

546
00:43:36,361 --> 00:43:38,080
of empire, of Rome

547
00:43:38,081 --> 00:43:40,780
most of all it has been a symbol of cruelty

548
00:43:40,781 --> 00:43:41,481
and death

549
00:43:41,482 --> 00:43:44,760
to this day the Pope leads an annual Easter

550
00:43:44,761 --> 00:43:46,140
procession through the stadium

551
00:43:46,141 --> 00:43:48,680
in memory of those who died within

552
00:43:48,681 --> 00:43:49,381
its walls

553
00:43:49,382 --> 00:43:53,460
death penalty opponents have also laid claim to the Colosseum

554
00:43:53,461 --> 00:43:57,840
rallying here anytime any country in the world renounces capital punishment

555
00:43:57,841 --> 00:44:02,620
as mock gladiators continue to perform at its feet

556
00:44:02,621 --> 00:44:07,520
the ancient stadium strangely does not seem out of place in the heart of modern Rome

557
00:44:07,521 --> 00:44:10,320
so large, so old

558
00:44:10,321 --> 00:44:13,720
it has become finally a symbol of time itself

559
00:44:13,721 --> 00:44:17,940
a kind of colossal sculpture that invites us to contemplate

560
00:44:17,941 --> 00:44:19,580
its many meanings.

